>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `Excl`

``` hol4
simpLib.Excl : string -> thm
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Excludes named rewrites, conversions and decision procedures for one
invocation.

`Excl name` is a directive in the theorem list of a simplification
conversion or tactic. It uses exactly the same strict name parser and
rule deletion as `ss -* [name]`. Qualification uses dots, not the kernel
`Thy$Name` spelling:

- A name without dots is unqualified.
- A two-part dotted name with an empty or all-digit second part is also
  unqualified (for example, `name.` or `name.1`); the full string is kept.
  Otherwise its first part must be an ancestor theory; an unknown theory
  raises `bad theory name`.
- A three-part dotted name is read as `Thy.Name.suffix` without an
  ancestry check. More dots raise `User key has too many dots`.

Deletion filters the installed rewrite/conversion net, not a source-theorem
inventory. For a parsed name `n`, an entry matches if its stored name is
`n`, `rewrite:n`, or begins with `rewrite:n.`, and its theory matches when
one was supplied. Named decision procedures are removed by exact string
equality with the original, unparsed string.

There is no namespace interpretation or strategy fallback: `rule:`,
`looper:`, `solver:`, `split:` and `case:` have no special meaning in an
`Excl` string. In particular, `Excl "bool$AND_CLAUSES"` does not exclude
the rewrite `bool.AND_CLAUSES`. Ordinary `simpLib` has no looper, solver or
split-strategy state to delete. Use [`simpExtLib.ExclX`](#simpExtLib.ExclX)
with an explicitly constructed extension simpset for extended exclusions.

An unmatched name is silent, but still records the same deletion history
as `-*`; it is not discarded just because no current rule matches.
Strict names are reparsed when that history is replayed, in the then-current
theory context (including after `Context.restore`). A later addition may
reinstall a deleted rule; this is not a prohibition on future additions.

### Failure

The constructor never fails. Processing the directive can fail for an
invalid name under the strict parser, just as `-*` can. Unmatched names
are not errors.

### Example

``` hol4
>> SIMP_CONV bool_ss [Excl "bool$AND_CLAUSES"] “T ∧ p”;
>> QCONV (SIMP_CONV bool_ss [Excl "bool.AND_CLAUSES"]) “T ∧ p”;
>> SIMP_CONV bool_ss [Excl "name."] “T ∧ p”;
```

